    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d44-a1">

                <pb facs="0185" n="67" sameAs="frus:frus1889/pg67"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure 1 in No. 23.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Adams to Mr.
                            Bocayuva.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Petropolis</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1889-11-25"><hi rendition="#italic">November</hi> 25,
                        1889</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I see it stated that the Provisional
                    Government contemplates dispensing with, the requirement that foreigners must
                    procure passports before leaving Brazil. Allow me to express the hope to your
                    excellency that the Government may accomplish this i proposed measure of relief
                    I know of nothing that would tend to facilitate more the trade of the ports of
                    Brazil than this free entrance and exit of the merchants of other countries,
                    many of whom now are often harassed by the delay caused in procuring passports
                    and the necessary vise”; sometimes to the extent of missing their; steamers.</p>
                <p>The extension of this liberty to foreigners would be in harmony with the spirit
                    of freedom that has so recently overspread your beautiful country.</p>
                <p>With expressions, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Robert Adams</hi>, Jr.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
